sections.body:
`document.querySelector("selector")` gives the **first** element that matches a CSS selector:

```js
const title = document.querySelector("#title");    // the one with id="title"
const note = document.querySelector(".note");      // the first one with class="note"
const firstItem = document.querySelector("ul li"); // the first li inside ul
```

To get **all** the matches, use `querySelectorAll`. It returns a list that you can walk through with `for...of` or `forEach`:

```js
const planets = document.querySelectorAll(".planet");
console.log(planets.length);
for (const p of planets) {
  console.log(p.textContent);
}
```

If nothing matches the selector, `querySelector` returns `null`. If you see the error `Cannot set properties of null`, check the selector: did you put `#` for an id and `.` for a class?

